> are in the bottom <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">11<sup>st</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). <br>Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability.","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"abstract\" </i></span> tend to be published in lower impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">33<sup>rd</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">71<sup>st</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"abstract\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">16<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}